WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.380
In the previous video we have seen how atomic
mass is measured.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.300
Now let us
understand atomic mass in detail.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:18.780
Before that, can you tell me what exactly
the concept of Atomic Number is?

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.900
Can you define it once for me?

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:21.900
Yes!

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.990
It is simply the number of protons present
inside the nucleus of an atom.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:31.339
Now on similar lines, can you tell me what
atomic mass could be?

00:00:31.339 --> 00:00:32.339
Yes!

00:00:32.339 --> 00:00:35.240
It simply means the mass of an atom.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.370
But how will we determine it?

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:42.130
Imagine we have a bag which is made up of
extremely thin material.

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:45.270
So thin
that it almost does not have any mass of its

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:46.760
own.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.520
Now we fill this bag with
some stuff inside.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.210
If this bag is placed on a weighing scale,
then can we say that

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:58.510
the total mass of the bag will be the sum
of mass of all the objects inside it?

00:00:58.510 --> 00:00:59.510
Absolutely!

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:05.080
Similarly, the mass of an atom can be calculated
with the help of the subatomic

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.180
particles in it.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:13.310
But we know that among all the subatomic particles
electrons have negligible

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:14.810
mass.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.670
That is they are considered to be almost weightless!

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.680
So what are we left with?

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:21.680
Yes!

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.000
We have both: protons and neutrons in the
nucleus.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.520
And these two do have some amount of mass
is what we know.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.240
Now tell me one thing, which among these two
particles should be

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.060
considered?

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.760
Shall we consider only the mass of protons
or only the neutrons?

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:40.960
Or both together?

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.550
Well, since both have a significant amount
of mass, we will

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.470
consider the mass of both together!

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:54.270
Thus, atomic mass is defined as the total
number of protons and neutrons

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.700
present inside the nucleus of the atom.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.400
It is also referred to as the mass
number!

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.320
Let us take a simple example.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.069
In case of a simple element like Fluorine,
we find that there are nine protons

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:11.120
and ten neutrons in the nucleus.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:16.469
Wait a second… we have always come across
cases where the atomic nucleus

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:19.349
has an equal number of protons and neutrons.

00:02:19.349 --> 00:02:23.489
So how is it that the number of
neutrons is more?

00:02:23.489 --> 00:02:27.810
Well, tell me, does the presence of additional
neutrons

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.249
really bother the atom?

00:02:30.249 --> 00:02:32.999
Does the charge of the atom increase or decrease?

00:02:32.999 --> 00:02:34.760
Absolutely not!

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.599
That’s because they’re neutral.

00:02:37.599 --> 00:02:40.689
Then why should an extra
neutron bother us?

00:02:40.689 --> 00:02:45.489
So coming back to the atomic mass of Fluorine,
we have nine protons and ten

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:47.249
neutrons.

00:02:47.249 --> 00:02:50.139
That means the total would give us nineteen!

00:02:50.139 --> 00:02:55.310
So can we say that the
atomic mass of Fluorine is nineteen units?

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:56.849
Yes!

00:02:56.849 --> 00:03:01.239
Just like Fluorine, even Aluminium has an
extra neutron compared to the

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:02.549
number of protons.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:08.980
That is, in a single atom of aluminium, we
have thirteen protons and fourteen

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.390
neutrons.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.129
Summing up the two we get twenty seven units
which is the atomic

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:15.269
mass of Aluminium.

00:03:15.269 --> 00:03:19.799
Now let’s take another random example of
Chlorine!

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:25.379
A chlorine atom has seventeen protons and
eighteen neutrons.

00:03:25.379 --> 00:03:28.549
That means the
atomic mass of chlorine will be thirty five

00:03:28.549 --> 00:03:29.549
units.

00:03:29.549 --> 00:03:30.549
Right?

00:03:30.549 --> 00:03:31.549
Absolutely!

00:03:31.549 --> 00:03:35.209
But wait a
second… a few chlorine atoms that exist

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:39.019
in nature exhibit a different form.

00:03:39.019 --> 00:03:41.349
)
Some of them have seventeen protons and twenty

00:03:41.349 --> 00:03:42.980
neutrons!

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:47.269
That gives us the
atomic mass of chlorine as thirty seven units!

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:49.840
So which one is the correct atomic mass?

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.489
In case of chlorine, both the atomic
masses are right.

00:03:54.489 --> 00:03:57.709
The atoms having seventeen protons and eighteen
neutrons

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:01.099
have an atomic mass of thirty five units.

00:04:01.099 --> 00:04:05.799
On the other hand, chlorine atoms
with seventeen protons and twenty neutrons

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:09.169
have an atomic mass of thirty
seven units.

00:04:09.169 --> 00:04:13.389
But now the next question to pop up in our
minds is “How can we have one

00:04:13.389 --> 00:04:18.829
element having two different kinds of atoms?”And
is this valid only for chlorine

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:22.690
or are there other elements also which exhibit
this phenomenon?

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:28.060
Let us find out more about this mysterious
concept of Isotopes in the next

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.510
video!

